-- Passage rg11-comparing-texts-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('17d5ac83-741c-52bc-8044-c84883e7974d', 'rg11-comparing-texts-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Telegraph Arrives (1870s)$q$, $q$Text 1: The Wire Comes to Halden (a story, 1874)

In the spring of 1874, the telegraph line reached Halden, and Orrin Baptiste was hired to run the small office beside the feed store. Before the wire, news from the capital came by stagecoach once a week, already stale by the time anyone read it. Now Orrin could tap out a question in the morning and have an answer before lunch.

The townspeople lined up at his window. A farmer asked for grain prices in the city. A mother sent word to a son working on the coast. The schoolteacher, Miss Adaeze Korr, requested the results of a spelling contest held three counties away, so her students could compare their scores.

Orrin loved the clicking key. Still, he noticed that people lingered less at the general store, where they had once traded news slowly over coffee. One evening old Teodor Vance stopped by the office and remarked that the town now knew everything faster but talked about it less. Orrin thought about this as he locked up. The next morning he hung a slate board by the door and began chalking up each day's news, so that neighbors would have a reason to stand together and read it aloud.

Text 2: Lines on the Wire (a poem, 1874)

A thread of iron crosses the hills,
And words run along it like quick light.
The miller hears the price of wheat by noon.
The far city feels next door tonight.

Yet the porch where stories once were spun
Stands quieter now at the close of day.
The news arrives before the walker does,
And no one waits to hear what travelers say.

O humming wire, you shorten every mile,
But mind the distance you cannot repair:
The pause between a question and its answer,
When neighbors gathered close and lingered there.$q$, 'draft')
