-- Passage rg11-comparing-texts-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2cbfa2e0-a6cf-58c2-83c9-acab8782d163', 'rg11-comparing-texts-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Railway Reaches the Valley (1850s)$q$, $q$Text 1: The Morning Train (a story, 1853)

I was fifteen in 1853 when the railway reached our valley. On the first morning, the whole town of Millbrook stood along the new tracks while the town band played slightly out of tune. When the engine rounded the bend, smoke rising like a gray flag, I felt the ground tremble under my boots.

I worked as an apprentice in Mr. Hadley's print shop, and within a month the train had changed my days. Orders came from the city twice a week instead of twice a season. Paper arrived cheaper and whiter than any we had seen. Best of all, the train brought a crate of new books for the lending shelf at the back of the shop, and I read every one.

My aunt lived ninety miles east, a journey that once took four days by wagon. Now it took one afternoon. That summer I visited her twice, watching fields slide past the window like pages turned by an impatient hand. The valley that had seemed like the whole world became one stop on a long line, and I was glad of it.

Text 2: The Last Barge (a story, 1853)

For thirty years Silas Okafor had guided cargo barges along the Millbrook canal, leading mules and boats through the locks at a patient walking pace. He knew every bend by the shape of its willows. In 1853, when the railway opened beside the canal, he watched the first train pass him in less time than it took to tie a rope.

By autumn, the merchants who had once hired him sent their goods by rail. The canal grew quiet, and weeds crept along the towpath. Silas did not curse the train. He admitted that a farmer could now sell apples in the city before they bruised, and that was a good thing.

Still, on his final trip, he let the barge drift slowly past the willows and named each lock aloud for his young helper, Benedek. "Somebody should remember how it was done," he said. Benedek wrote the names in a small notebook, and Silas nodded as if a heavy load had been set safely ashore.$q$, 'draft')
