-- Passage rg11-comparing-texts-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('aa3d9918-1a09-5b51-9564-9a23ceabc5f9', 'rg11-comparing-texts-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Music for Silent Films (1910s)$q$, $q$Text 1: The Piano in the Dark (a story, 1915)

Every evening at seven, Ines Takahara climbed the three steps to the piano below the screen at the Starlight Hall. She had no written music for most films. Instead she watched the flickering pictures and chose her music in the moment: a quick march when the hero chased a runaway streetcar, a soft waltz when two old friends met again, a comic tumble of notes when a dog stole a gentleman's hat.

Her hands ached by the second show. Sometimes the film reel broke, and she played a cheerful tune for the audience while the projectionist, Mr. Abara, mended it with tape. She knew that without her the pictures were only gray shapes moving in silence. With her, the room breathed together.

One night a small boy came to the front after the show. "How did you know the dog would steal the hat?" he asked. Ines laughed. She had not known. She had simply been watching as closely as he had.

Text 2: Matinee (a poem, 1915)

The lamps go dim, the curtain parts,
The silver pictures start.
No actor speaks a single word,
Yet something speaks to my heart.

Below the screen, a player sits
Whose name I never learn.
Her music runs beside the film
Like wind beside a candle's burn.

When the rider gallops, so do the keys.
When the lost child cries, they sigh.
A hundred strangers in the dark
Feel the same notes pass by.

I leave the hall and step outside
Into the ordinary street,
But the tune keeps playing in my step
And follows my small feet.$q$, 'draft')
