-- Passage rg11-comparing-texts-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('10e9d0cb-9c22-509a-b6de-4a81b532ed66', 'rg11-comparing-texts-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Trees Against the Wind (1930s)$q$, $q$Text 1: Shelterbelt (a story, 1936)

For three summers the wind had lifted the topsoil from the Castellanos farm and carried it east in brown clouds. In the spring of 1936, a man from the county arrived with a wagon of bare-root saplings: cedar, hackberry and plum. He explained that rows of trees planted along the edge of a field would slow the wind and hold the soil in place.

Nadia Castellanos, who was thirteen, helped her father dig the holes. The saplings looked like sticks with roots. Their neighbor, Mr. Whitlock, leaned on the fence and asked how a few twigs could stop a wind that crossed three states. Her father answered that the twigs would not stay twigs.

All summer, Nadia carried buckets from the well to the row of trees each evening. Some saplings died, and she replanted them. By August, the plum trees had a few leaves, and the soil behind them felt slightly damper under her hand than the soil in the open field. It was not much. But she marked each tree's height on a stick, and every week the marks climbed a little higher.

Text 2: Planting for Later (a poem, 1936)

I set a twig into the ground
Where nothing grows but wind.
My neighbor laughs. He says the sky
Will take what I begin.

I water it at evening
When the dust has settled low.
I will not sit beneath its shade;
I plant it even so.

Some child I will never meet
Will walk this field one day,
And find the ground still holding fast
Where once it blew away.

So let the neighbor laugh his laugh.
The roots are slow and deep.
A promise made in small green leaves
Is still a promise kept.$q$, 'draft')
