-- Passage rg11-comparing-texts-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6ebe01bc-a726-5127-b68d-6177561a23f1', 'rg11-comparing-texts-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Watching the Moon Landing (1960s)$q$, $q$Text 1: The Neighbor's Television (a story, 1969)

On the night of the first moon landing, nineteen people squeezed into the Haddad family's living room, because theirs was the only television on Linden Court. Children sat cross-legged on the rug. The grown-ups stood along the walls holding paper plates of melon. Tomasz Ward, who was eleven, found a spot so close to the screen that he could see the tiny dots that made up the picture.

The image was blurry and gray, and for long minutes nothing seemed to happen. Mrs. Haddad's toddler fell asleep. Then a shape moved down a ladder, and a careful boot pressed into the dust. The room went completely silent. Nobody reached for melon. Even Mr. Petrakis, who argued about everything, said nothing at all.

Afterward, the neighbors spilled out onto the street and looked up. The moon hung over the rooftops, looking exactly as it always had. Tomasz could not believe it looked so ordinary. Somebody was standing on it right now. He stayed outside until his mother called him in twice, trying to spot a speck that he knew he could never see.

Text 2: Night Shift, July (a poem, 1969)

I heard it on the factory radio,
Between the clatter of the second shift.
They walked there. On the moon. Tonight.
The foreman stopped the line a minute,
And no one spoke. We only stood.

At midnight I walked home alone.
The moon was where it always was,
Low and white above the water tower.
I tried to picture boots upon it.
I could not, and I could.

What struck me most was not the moon.
It was the thought of them looking back
At this small blue place, our town on it somewhere,
My lunch pail, my front step, my porch light,
All of it riding through the dark together.$q$, 'draft')
